In that case, since the sets are small, a simple sorted vector may be the fastest approach. You can do the following:

1. Store each set of numbers in a Vec<u64>, sorted in ascending order.
2. To check disjointness, iterate the vectors simultaneously, comparing elements at the same index. 
3. If you ever find two equal elements, the sets are not disjoint. 
4. If you reach the end of either vector first, the sets are disjoint.

For example:
```rust
let mut set1 = vec![1, 3, 5];
set1.sort();

let mut set2 = vec![2, 4, 6]; 
set2.sort();

let disjoint = {
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = 0;
    while i < set1.len() && j < set2.len() {
        if set1[i] == set2[j] {
            return false; // Found equal elements, not disjoint
        } else if set1[i] < set2[j] {
            i += 1;
        } else {
            j += 1;
        }
    }
    // Reached end of one vector, sets are disjoint
    true 
};

assert!(disjoint);
```

The runtime of this approach will be O(N + M) where N and M are the sizes of the two sets. Since the sets are small, the constant factors of sorting and iterating the vector will likely make this faster than a HashSet for your use case.

