Instead of using file_put_contents() which overwrites the entire file, you can use file_get_contents() to read the existing file, make changes, and then write the file back with file_put_contents().

For example:

```php
// Read existing file
$content = file_get_contents('file.txt');

// Make changes to $content
$content = str_replace('old', 'new', $content);

// Write changed content back to file 
file_put_contents('file.txt', $content);
```

This will read the file.txt file, replace all instances of 'old' with 'new', and then write the file back, replacing the original content.

You can make any changes to the $content string before writing back to the file. Some other examples:

- Append content: `$content .= 'Some new content';`
- Remove content: `$content = substr($content, 0, -10);` 
- Encode/decode: `$content = json_encode($some_array);`
- And many more...

So a full example would be:

```php
$content = file_get_contents('data.json');
$data = json_decode($content, true);

$data['name'] = 'NAME_2 NAME_3';

$content = json_encode($data);
file_put_contents('data.json', $content); 
```

This will:

- Read data.json 
- Decode the JSON string to an array 
- Update the name property 
- Encode the array back to JSON
- And save it back to data.json, replacing the original content

So this is a good way to make changes to existing file content without overwriting the entire file.

