You can use if/else statements in PHP to conditionally "goto" different parts of a program.

For example:

```php
$age = 30;

if ($age > 18) {
    goto adult; 
}

echo 'Minor';
exit;

adult: 
echo 'Adult';
```

This will check if $age is greater than 18, and if so, jump to the adult label. So the output will be:

```
Adult
```

If $age was less than 18, the output would be:

```
Minor 
```

You can use goto to jump to any label within the same scope. Some other examples:

```php
$x = true;

if ($x) {
    goto end; 
} 

echo 'Hello';  

end:
echo 'World!'; 
```

Output: 
```
World!
```

We jump past the `echo 'Hello';` line and go straight to the end label.

```php 
for ($i = 0; $i < 10; $i++) {
    if ($i == 5) {
        goto end; 
    }
    echo $i; 
}

end: 
echo 'Done!';
```

Output:
```
01234Done!
```

We jump out of the for loop when $i is 5 and go to the end label.

While goto can be useful in some situations, it often makes code hard to read and follow. It is usually better to use structured constructs like if/else statements, loops, and functions. Goto should only be used sparingly in PHP.

