To follow the evolution of a React state variable, you can use the `setState` method provided by React. `setState` updates the state of the component and re-renders the component with the new state.

You can use the `console.log` method to log the previous and next state of the variable in the component's `render` method. Here's an example:
```javascript
class MyComponent extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = {
      count: 0
    };
  }

  componentDidMount() {
    console.log(this.state.count); // initial count
  }

  render() {
    console.log(this.state.count); // current count
    return (
      <div>
        <h1>Count: {this.state.count}</h1>
      </div>
    );
  }

  handleClick() {
    this.setState({
      count: this.state.count + 1
    });
  }
}
```
In this example, the `console.log` statement in the `render` method logs the current count of the state variable each time the component is rendered. The `console.log` statement in the `componentDidMount` method logs the initial count of the state variable when the component is first mounted.

By looking at the console output, you can see the evolution of the state variable over time and how it changes when the `handleClick` method is called.

