Here's a simple C++ program that creates a time clock that counts down from 120 seconds:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
#include <this_thread.h>

const int seconds_to_count = 120;

void countdown() {
    int remaining_seconds = seconds_to_count;

    while (remaining_seconds > 0) {
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));
        remaining_seconds--;
        std::cout << remaining_seconds << std::endl;
    }
}

int main() {
    std::thread t(countdown);
    t.detach(); // Detach the thread so it runs independently from the main thread

    // Do some other work here (e.g., print "Finished") after the countdown is done
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(120));
    std::cout << "Finished" << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```
This program creates a separate thread for the countdown process. The main thread sleeps for 120 seconds and then prints "Finished". You can replace the sleep and print statement in the main thread with your desired code.

