In C, you can use the `argv` array, which is a part of the `argc` argument passed to the main function, to get the names of the command-line arguments passed to the program, including the program name itself. The first element of `argv` will contain the name of the program, and subsequent elements will contain the names of any command-line arguments passed to the program.

You can use the following code snippet to print the name of the current program:
```c
int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    printf("Program name: %s\n", argv[0]);
    return 0;
}
```
This will print the name of the current program to the console. Keep in mind that if the program is run from a different location than where it is stored, the name of the program may be different, as it will include the path to the program.

You can store the name of the program in a variable by using the `argv[0]` index in the `argv` array. The `argv[0]` index contains the name of the program passed as the first command-line argument. Here's an example:
```c
int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char program_name[100];
    strcpy(program_name, argv[0]);
    printf("Program name: %s\n", program_name);
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, the `program_name` array is initialized to a buffer of 100 characters, and the `strcpy` function is used to copy the name of the program from the `argv[0]` index to the `program_name` array. After that, you can use the `program_name` variable to store the name of the program, or use it in any other way you want in your program.

You can make the size of the `program_name` array exact by specifying the size of the array using the `sizeof` operator, like this:
```c
int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char program_name[sizeof(argv[0])];
    strcpy(program_name, argv[0]);
    printf("Program name: %s\n", program_name);
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, the size of the `program_name` array is set to the size of the `argv[0]` string, which is the name of the program passed as the first command-line argument. The `sizeof` operator is used to determine the size of the `argv[0]` string, and the `char` array `program_name` is initialized with that size. This ensures that the `program_name` array is large enough to hold the name of the program, without any trailing null characters or other unnecessary space.

You can copy the name of the program to the `program_name` array without using the `strcpy` function by using the `memcpy` function, like this:
```c
int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char program_name[sizeof(argv[0])];
    memcpy(program_name, argv[0], sizeof(argv[0]));
    printf("Program name: %s\n", program_name);
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, the `program_name` array is initialized to a buffer of the same size as the name of the program passed as the first command-line argument. Then, the `memcpy` function is used to copy the name of the program from the `argv[0]` string to the `program_name` array. The `memcpy` function copies the specified number of bytes from the source string to the destination array, so it automatically handles any trailing null characters or other unnecessary space.

Note that the `memcpy` function only copies the characters of the string, it doesn't copy the null character that terminates the string, so you should add the null character manually after the copy.

