Here's an example of how to implement the singleton pattern in PHP:
```php
class Singleton {
    private static $instance = null;

    private function __construct() {
        if (self::$instance === null) {
            self::$instance = $this;
        }
    }

    public static function getInstance() {
        if (self::$instance === null) {
            self::$instance = new self();
        }
        return self::$instance;
    }
}
```
In this example, the `__construct` method checks if there is already an instance of the Singleton class created. If so, it simply returns the existing instance. If not, it creates a new instance of the class and assigns it to the `self::$instance` variable. The `getInstance` method simply returns the `self::$instance` variable, which will always contain the same instance of the class.

