Yes, Elasticsearch provides support for commit and rollback operations through its Transaction API.

When you begin a transaction in Elasticsearch, you can perform multiple operations on the index, such as indexing new documents, updating existing documents, or deleting documents. Once you have completed all the operations, you can call the "commit" method on the transaction to make the changes visible in the index. If any of the operations fail, you can call the "rollback" method to undo the changes and return the index to its previous state.

Here's an example of how you can use the Transaction API in Elasticsearch to perform a set of operations and commit or rollback the changes:
```java
// Begin a new transaction
Translog.Builder translogBuilder = getTranslog();
TransactionResponse response = translogBuilder.startTransaction();

// Index a new document
IndexRequest indexRequest = new IndexRequest("myindex");
indexRequest.source("{\"field\":\"value\"}");
IndexResponse indexResponse = client().index(indexRequest, RequestOptions.DEFAULT);

// Update an existing document
UpdateRequest updateRequest = new UpdateRequest("myindex", "1");
updateRequest.source("{\"field\":\"value\"}");
UpdateResponse updateResponse = client().update(updateRequest, RequestOptions.DEFAULT);

// Delete an existing document
DeleteRequest deleteRequest = new DeleteRequest("myindex", "2");
DeleteResponse deleteResponse = client().delete(deleteRequest, RequestOptions.DEFAULT);

// Commit the transaction
if (response.isSuccess()) {
    client().admin().indices().prepareCommit().setTransaction(response.getTransaction()).execute().actionGet();
}

// If an error occurs during any of the operations, rollback the transaction
if (response.isSuccess() == false) {
    client().admin().indices().prepareRollback().setTransaction(response.getTransaction()).execute().actionGet();
