The `system()` function in C is a built-in function that allows you to execute a shell command and return the exit status of that command. On Windows, you can use the `CreateProcess()` function to execute a command and obtain its exit code. Here's an example of how you could create a similar function using system calls on Windows:
```c
#include <windows.h>
#include <tchar.h>

int system(const char *command) {
    STARTUPINFO si;
    PROCESS_INFORMATION pi;

    ZeroMemory(&si, sizeof(si));
    si.cb = sizeof(si);
    ZeroMemory(&pi, sizeof(pi));

    if (!CreateProcess(NULL,   // No module name (use command line)
        command,   // Command line
        NULL,   // Process handle not inheritable
        NULL,   // Thread handle not inheritable
        FALSE,  // Set handle inheritance to FALSE
        0,     // No creation flags
        NULL,   // Use parent's environment block
        NULL,   // Use parent's starting directory
        &si,    // Pointer to STARTUPINFO structure
        &pi))   // Pointer to PROCESS_INFORMATION structure
    {
        return -1;
    }

    // Wait until child process exits.
    WaitForSingleObject(pi.hProcess, INFINITE);

    // Close process and thread handles.
    CloseHandle(pi.hProcess);
    CloseHandle(pi.hThread);

    return (int)GetExitCodeProcess(pi.hProcess);
}
```
The `CreateProcess()` function has similar parameters to the `system()` function, allowing you to pass in the command to be executed as the first parameter. The second parameter is the command line, which is used if the first parameter is NULL. The `CreateProcess()` function returns a handle to the child process and the `WaitForSingleObject()` function is used to wait until the process exits. Finally, the `CloseHandle()` function is used to close the handles to the process and thread, and the exit code can be obtained using the `GetExitCodeProcess()` function.

