```
2. Create a test class and define the static method you want to mock:
```java
public class MyClass {
    public static void myStaticMethod() {
        System.out.println("Hello from myStaticMethod");
    }
}
```
3. In your test class, call `Mockito.mockStatic` and pass in the class you want to mock and the name of the static method:
```java
import mockito.Mockito;

public class MyClassTest {
    @Before
    public void setUp() {
        Mockito.mockStatic(MyClass.class);
    }

    @Test
    public void testMyStaticMethod() {
        MyClass.myStaticMethod();
        // Verify that the static method was called once
        Mockito.verify(Mockito.times(1)).myStaticMethod();
    }
}
```
In this example, we create a test class for `MyClass` and define a `setUp` method that calls `Mockito.mockStatic` to mock the `MyClass` static methods. We then call `myStaticMethod` and use `Mockito.verify` to verify that it was called once.

By using `Mockito.mockStatic`, you can control the behavior of the static method and return values that you want to test against. This is useful when you want to isolate a static method from its dependencies and test it in isolation.

