Here is an example of a quicksort implementation in C++:
```c
void quicksort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
  if (low < high) {
    int pivot = partition(arr, low, high);
    quicksort(arr, low, pivot - 1);
    quicksort(arr, pivot + 1, high);
  }
}

int partition(int arr[], int low, int high) {
  int pivot = arr[high];
  int i = low - 1;
  for (int j = low; j <= high; j++) {
    if (arr[j] <= pivot) {
      i++;
      swap(arr[i], arr[j]);
    }
  }
  swap(arr[i + 1], arr[high]);
  return i + 1;
}
```
This implementation uses the "Lomuto partition scheme" which is one of the most efficient partition schemes for quicksort. The `quicksort` function takes an array, a low index, and a high index as input, and partitions the array using the partition function, then recursively sorts the partitioned sub-arrays. The `partition` function takes an array, a low index, and a high index as input, and uses the pivot element to partition the array, then recursively sorts the partitioned sub-arrays.

Please note that this is a simple implementation of quicksort, and it may not be the most efficient implementation for large arrays. Also, this implementation does not handle the case when the pivot element is equal to one of the elements in the array, in that case the pivot should be moved to the left or right.

