In Svelte, there are two types of rendering: server-side rendering (SSR) and client-side rendering (CSR).

Server-side rendering (SSR) is a type of rendering where the server generates the HTML and sends it to the client. This means that Svelte templates are rendered on the server, and the generated HTML is returned to the client as a response to an HTTP request.

Here's an example of how to use server-side rendering in a Svelte application using Sveltekit:


```javascript
import { createApp } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import svelte from '@sveltejs/kit/svelte.js';

const app = createApp({
  template: '<div>Hello, {name}}!</div>',
  name: 'NAME_1
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000.');
});

```

In this example, the `` template `` attribute of the `` createApp `` config object defines the Svelte template that will be rendered on the server. The `` name `` attribute is used to provide dynamic content to the template, which will be replaced by the value of the `` name `` variable at runtime.

Client-side rendering (CSR) is a type of rendering where the client generates the HTML using the Svelte template. This means that Svelte templates are rendered on the client, and the generated HTML is sent to the server as an AJAX request.

Here's an example of how to use client-side rendering in a Svelte application using Sveltekit:


```javascript
import { createApp } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import svelte from '@sveltejs/kit/svelte.js';

const app = createApp({
  template: '<div>Hello, {name}}!</div>',
  name: 'NAME_2'
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  const name = 'NAME_2';
  const html = `<div>Hello, ${name}!</div>`;
  fetch('/', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify({ html }),
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json'
    }
  })
    .then(response => {
      if (response.ok) {
        const data = await response.json();
        console.log(data.html);
      } else {
        console.log('Error: Could not render template on the client.');
      }
    })
    .catch(error => {
      console.log('Error: Could not render template on the client.');
    });
});

```

In this example, the `` template `` attribute of the `` createApp `` config object is used to define the Svelte template that will be rendered on the client. The `` name `` attribute is used to provide dynamic content to the template, which will be replaced by the value of the `` name `` variable at runtime. The `` fetch `` function is used to send an AJAX request to the server with the generated HTML as the request body. The `` then `` and `` catch `` functions are used to handle the response from the server. If the response is successful, the generated HTML will be logged to the console. If the response is not successful, an error will be logged to the console.

In summary, server-side rendering (SSR) and client-side rendering (CSR) are two different types of rendering in Svelte. SSR is where the server generates the HTML, and CSR is where the client generates the HTML using the Svelte template.

