To remove a tun interface via ioctl API, you can use the `ioctl` function and pass the appropriate command to the interface.

For example, if you want to bring down the tun interface, you can use the `TUNSETIF` ioctl command with the `TUN_CMD_IFDOWN` option. Here is an example code snippet:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>
#include <net/if.h>
#include <linux/tun.h>

int main()
{
    int tun_fd, ret;
    char cmd[64];

    // Create the tun interface
    tun_fd = open("/dev/net/tun", O_RDWR | O_NONBLOCK);
    if (tun_fd < 0) {
        printf("Error opening tun device\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // Set up the interface parameters
    memset(cmd, 0, sizeof(cmd));
    strncpy(cmd, "ifname", sizeof(cmd) - 1);
    strncpy(cmd + sizeof(cmd) - 8, "tun0", sizeof(cmd) - 1 - 8);

    // Bring down the interface
    ret = ioctl(tun_fd, TUNSETIF, cmd);
    if (ret < 0) {
        perror("TUNSETIF");
        close(tun_fd);
        return 1;
    }

    // Close the interface
    close(tun_fd);

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we first create a tun interface using the `open` function. We then set up the `TUNSETIF` command using the `memset` and `strncpy` functions to set the `ifname` parameter to the name of the interface, and the `cmd` parameter to the tuning knob command.

Finally, we pass the `TUNSETIF` command to the interface using the `ioctl` function. This brings down the interface. We then close the interface using the `close` function.

