Lazy loading in Laravel Orchid works by only loading relationships and data when specifically requested. This helps improve performance by not loading unnecessary data up front.

Some examples of how lazy loading is used in Orchid:

1. Relationships - When you define a relationship on an Orchid model, like `belongsTo`, the related model is not actually loaded until you access that relationship. For example:

```php
$user = User::find(1);

// Related model is not loaded yet
$user->NAME_2; 

// Now the related NAME_2 is loaded
echo $user->NAME_2->name; 
```

2. Table fields - When displaying a table in Orchid, not all columns are loaded up front. Only the columns that are displayed in the current view are loaded. So if you have 20 columns but only show 5 at a time, only those 5 columns are loaded from the database.

3. Modal fields - When you open an Orchid modal to edit an item, only the fields that are actually used in that modal are loaded from the database. So if you have a modal to edit just a few fields of a model with many columns, only those few fields are loaded.

4. Tabs - The content of Orchid tabs is not loaded until you actually click on that tab. This avoids loading unnecessary data for tabs that a user may never even click on.

So in summary, lazy loading in Orchid helps optimize performance by only loading relationships, fields, and data that are specifically needed for the current view. Everything else is left unloaded until explicitly requested.

Lazy loading works a bit differently for select fields in Orchid. Since a select needs to show all options to the user up front, it can't lazy load those options.

However, what it can do is lazy load the relationship data for the options, if they are related to another model. For example:

Say you have a User model and a NAME_2 model, with a `belongsTo` relationship:

```php
class User extends Orchid\Platform\Models\User
{
    public function NAME_2()
    {
        return $this->belongsTo(NAME_2::class);
    }
}

class NAME_2 extends Orchid\Platform\Models\NAME_2
{
    // ...
}
```

In your Orchid screen, you add a `select` field to edit the user's `NAME_2_id`:

```php
$user->select('NAME_2_id')
    ->fromModel(NAME_2::class, 'name')
```

Even though this shows all the NAME_2 options in the select, it does not actually eager load the `NAME_2` relationship on those options. So if you did:

```php 
$roles = NAME_2::all();
foreach ($roles as $NAME_2) {
    echo $NAME_2->someRelationship; 
}
```

It would make a query for each NAME_2 to load that relationship.

However, once a user selects a NAME_2 in that Orchid select field, that specific NAME_2's relationships are then loaded, since Orchid assumes you will likely need to access them.

So in summary, for selects Orchid can't lazy load the options themselves, but it can lazy load the relationships on those options until one is specifically selected.

