```c++
#include <cmath>

void NAME_1(int n, int m) {
  int i;
  int j;
  int k;
  int temp;
  int a = n;
  int b = m;

  if (b == 0) {
    return;
  }

  // Find the NAME_2 power of 2 that is less than a/2
  int powerOf2 = 0;
  while ((a & 1) == 0) {
    powerOf2++;
    a >>= 1;
  }

  // Use the power of 2 to evenly divide a and b
  a = a / powerOf2;
  b = b / powerOf2;

  // Calculate the cofactors
  for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    temp = a * b;
    a = temp / a;
    b = temp % a;
  }

  // Re-assemble the matrix
  for (j = 0; j < m; j++) {
    for (k = 0; k < n; k++) {
      temp = a * b;
      a = temp / a;
      b = temp % a;
      result[k][j] = result[k][j] + temp;
    }
  }
}
```

This implementation uses a divide-and-conquer approach to solve the NAME_1 algorithm. It first finds the maximum power of 2 that is less than the ratio of the lengths of the two matrices (a/2). It then evenly divides each matrix by this power of 2 and calculates the cofactors. Finally, it reassembles the matrix using a nested loop.

