Here is an example of a binary search algorithm written in C:
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int binary_search(int arr[], int l, int r, int x) {
  if (r >= l) {
    int mid = l + (r - l) / 2;
    if (arr[mid] == x)
      return mid;
    if (arr[mid] > x)
      return binary_search(arr, l, mid - 1, x);
    return binary_search(arr, mid + 1, r, x);
  }
  return -1;
}

int main() {
  int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6};
  int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
  int result;

  result = binary_search(arr, 0, n - 1, 4);
  if (result == -1)
    printf("Element is not present in array\n");
  else
    printf("Element is present at index %d\n", result);

  return 0;
}
```
This implementation of binary search uses the following steps:

1. Start with the left and right indices, and calculate the middle index.
2. Check if the element is present at the middle index.
3. If it is, return the middle index.
4. If it is not, check if the element is greater than the middle element.
5. If it is, search the left half of the array.
6. If it is not, search the right half of the array.
7. Repeat steps 4-6 until the element is found or the indices are out of range.

In this example, the binary search is performed on the array `arr` for the element `4`. If the element is not found, the function returns -1. Otherwise, it returns the index of the element in the array.

