In the first example, we create a new `XMLHttpRequest` object and set the HTTP method to GET. We also set the request's `onload` handler to execute when the response is received. In the `onload` handler, we check the response status and print the response text if the status is 200 (indicating a successful request). Finally, we call the `send()` method to send the request.

In the second example, we use the `fetch()` function to make the HTTP request. We pass the URL of the resource we want to fetch as the first argument. The `then()` method is called when the request is successful, and we get the response text using the `text()` method. We then log the response text to the console. The `catch()` method is called if there is an error with the request, and we log the error to the console.

Note that the `fetch()` function is supported in modern browsers, but `XMLHttpRequest` is supported in all browsers.

