Microsoft.Data.SqlClient is the recommended SQL Client library for .NET Core and .NET Standard applications. Some reasons to use Microsoft.Data.SqlClient over System.Data.SqlClient are:

- It's built to be cross-platform, so it works on .NET Core which runs on Linux, macOS and Windows. System.Data.SqlClient only works on Windows.
- It has better performance than System.Data.SqlClient.
- It supports newer SQL Server features that System.Data.SqlClient does not.

Example code using Microsoft.Data.SqlClient:
```csharp
using Microsoft.Data.SqlClient;

string connectionString = "Data Source=localhost;Initial Catalog=databaseName;Integrated Security=True";

using (SqlConnection connection = new SqlConnection(connectionString))
{
    connection.Open();

    string query = "SELECT * FROM TableName";

    using (SqlCommand command = new SqlCommand(query, connection))
    {
        using (SqlDataReader reader = command.ExecuteReader())
        {
            while (reader.Read())
            {
                Console.WriteLine(reader["ColumnName"]);    
            }    
        }
    }
}
```

The code is very similar to the previous example, but uses Microsoft.Data.SqlClient instead of System.Data.SqlClient.

The main differences are:

- Use Microsoft.Data.SqlClient namespace
- Use SqlConnection, SqlCommand and SqlDataReader from Microsoft.Data.SqlClient

So in summary, Microsoft recommends Microsoft.Data.SqlClient over System.Data.SqlClient for .NET Core and .NET Standard applications due to its cross-platform support, performance and newer SQL Server feature support.

