Here is a C++ program that disables input echo for the terminal:

```cpp
#include <termios.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    struct termios oldt, newt;

    // Get the terminal attributes
    tcgetattr(STDIN_FILENO, &oldt);

    // Make a copy of the old attributes
    newt = oldt;

    // Disable echo
    newt.c_lflag &= ~ECHO;

    // Set the new attributes
    tcsetattr(STDIN_FILENO, TCSANOW, &newt);

    // Read input
    char input;
    read(STDIN_FILENO, &input, 1);

    // Restore old attributes
    tcsetattr(STDIN_FILENO, TCSANOW, &oldt);
}
```

This program does the following:

1. Gets the current terminal attributes using tcgetattr() and stores it in oldt.
2. Makes a copy of the old attributes in newt.
3. Disables the ECHO flag in newt to disable input echo. 
4. Sets the new attributes using tcsetattr(). This disables echo.
5. Reads a single character of input.
6. Restores the old terminal attributes to re-enable echo.

So this program will disable echo, read a single character of input, and then re-enable echo.

