```javascript
var startTime = performance.now();
// code to measure
var endTime = performance.now();
console.log("Code executed in " + (endTime - startTime) + " milliseconds");
```
1. Use a profiling library: There are several profiling libraries available for JavaScript that can help you profile your code. For example, the popular `cluster` library provides a simple way to profile Node.js applications.

Profiling your JavaScript code can help you identify performance bottlenecks and optimize your code for better performance. It's important to keep in mind that profiling can be resource-intensive, so it's a good idea to profile your code in stages and only when necessary.

