The try-catch syntax is used in JavaScript to handle errors that may occur in a block of code. It is typically used when you need to perform an operation that may cause an error, and you want to handle that error gracefully instead of letting it crash your application.

Here's an example of how you might use try-catch in a JavaScript application:
```javascript
try {
  // code that may cause an error
  var x = 5 / y;
  console.log(x);
} catch (error) {
  // code to handle the error
  console.log("Error: " + error.message);
}
```
In this example, we have a division operation that may cause a `ZeroDivisionError` if `y` is 0. We use the try-catch syntax to catch that error and handle it by logging an error message to the console.

You can also specify multiple catch blocks to handle different types of errors. For example:
```javascript
try {
  // code that may cause an error
  var x = 5 / y;
  console.log(x);
} catch (error1) {
  // code to handle error1
  console.log("Error: " + error1.message);
} catch (error2) {
  // code to handle error2
  console.log("Error: " + error2.message);
}
```
In this example, we have two catch blocks to handle different types of errors. The first catch block handles `ZeroDivisionError`s, while the second catch block handles any other type of error.

Overall, the try-catch syntax is a useful tool for handling errors in JavaScript applications. It allows you to write more robust code that can handle unexpected situations gracefully, instead of crashing or displaying error messages to the user.

