Here is a C++ program that calculates the number of set bits (population count) in a given integer:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <climits>

int population_count(unsigned int n) {
  int count = 0;
  while (n > 0) {
    n &= n - 1;
    count++;
  }
  return count;
}

int main() {
  unsigned int n;
  std::cout << "Enter an integer: ";
  std::cin >> n;
  std::cout << "The population count of " << n << " is " << population_count(n) << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
```

Explanation:
The population_count function takes an unsigned integer n as input and returns the number of set bits in n. The function uses a while loop to repeatedly clear the least significant bit that is set and increment the count. The & operator is a bitwise AND operation, which sets the least significant bit to 0 if it is not already set. The n - 1 expression is used to ensure that only the least significant bit is cleared. The count variable is incremented after each iteration of the while loop.

The main function displays a prompt to the user to enter an integer and calls the population_count function to calculate the population count of the input integer. The result is then displayed to the user.

