You can use file() and explode() to read a file line by line into an array in PHP:

```php
$lines = [];
$file = fopen("file.txt", "r");
while ($line = fgets($file)) {
    $lines[] = $line;
}
fclose($file);

// $lines is now an array with each line of the file as an element
```

This opens the file, reads it line by line with fgets(), and adds each line to the $lines array.

You can also use file_get_contents() and explode():

```php 
$content = file_get_contents("file.txt");
$lines = explode("\n", $content);
```

This reads the entire file into a string with file_get_contents(), then splits that string into lines using \n as the delimiter.

You can use explode() again to split each line on //, and store the parts in variables:

```php
$lines = [];
$file = fopen("file.txt", "r");
while ($line = fgets($file)) {
    $parts = explode("//", $line);
    $before = $parts[0];
    $after = $parts[1];
    $lines[] = [
        "before" => $before,
        "after" => $after
    ];
}
fclose($file);

// $lines is now an array of: 
// [
//     "before" => "part before //", 
//     "after" => "part after //"
// ]
```

This will split each line on //, store the part before in $before, and the part after in $after. It then adds an associative array with those two values to the $lines array.

So if your file.txt contained:

```
Hello // World 
How // Are 
You // Today
``` 

$lines would be:

```
[
    ["before" => "Hello ", "after" => " World"], 
    ["before" => "How ", "after" => " Are"],
    ["before" => "You ", "after" => " Today"]
]
```

