In a Laravel project, enums can be defined in a few different ways, depending on the desired usage and organization. Here are a few options:

1. Define the enum as a class method within the class that will use the enum. For example:
```php
class ExampleEnum
{
    public static function getValues()
    {
        return [
            'value1',
            'value2',
            'value3',
        ];
    }
}
```
You can then use the values from the enum like this:
```php
$value = ExampleEnum::getValues()[1];
```
2. Define the enum as a const within the class that will use the enum. For example:
```php
class ExampleEnum
{
    const VALUES = [
        'value1',
        'value2',
        'value3',
    ];
}
```
You can then use the values from the enum like this:
```php
$value = self::VALUES[1];
```
3. Define the enum as a separate constant at the top level of your application. For example:
```php
const VALUES = [
    'value1',
    'value2',
    'value3',
];
```
You can then use the values from the enum like this:
```php
$value = VALUES[1];
```
In general, it's a good idea to keep enums defined in a separate location, such as a constants file, to make it easier to maintain and update them if needed. You can also use a package like Laravel Scout to define and search enums in a more powerful and flexible way.

Here is a basic Java Servlet that can perform speech segmentation:
```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

import javax.servlet.ServletException;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServlet;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;

import edu.cmu.sphinx.api.Configuration;
import edu.cmu.sphinx.api.LiveSpeechRecognizer;
import edu.cmu.sphinx.api.SpeechResult;

public class SpeechSegmentationServlet extends HttpServlet {
    private LiveSpeechRecognizer recognizer;

    public void init() throws ServletException {
        // Create a speech recognizer
        Configuration configuration = new Configuration();
        configuration.setAcousticModelPath("resource:/edu/cmu/sphinx/models/en-us/en-us");
        configuration.setDictionaryPath("resource:/edu/cmu/sphinx/models/en-us/cmudict-en-us.dict");
        recognizer = new LiveSpeechRecognizer(configuration);
    }

    public void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) 
            throws ServletException, IOException {
        // Get the audio data from the request
        byte[] audio = request.getInputStream().readAllBytes(); 

        // Recognize speech and get the result
        SpeechResult result = recognizer.recognize(audio);
        List<String> words = new ArrayList<>();
        for (edu.cmu.sphinx.result.WordResult wordResult : result.getWords()) {
            words.add(wordResult.getWord().toLowerCase());
        }

        // Segment into sentences
        List<List<String>> sentences = segmentIntoSentences(words);

        // Return the sentences in JSON format
        response.setContentType("application/json");
        response.getWriter().print(sentencesToJson(sentences));
    }
}
```

This servlet initializes a LiveSpeechRecognizer from the Sphinx speech recognition library. When it receives an audio POST request, it recognizes the speech and gets a list of words. It then segments the words into sentences using a basic sentence segmentation algorithm. Finally it returns the list of sentences in JSON format.

