Here's a JavaScript function that calculates an array of entries between two timestamps for a given time frame and selects time units greater than the given time frame:


```javascript
function calculateEntries(start, end, timeFrame) {
  const units = Math.floor(timeFrame * (Math.log10(Math.abs(end - start)) + 1));
  const startIndex = Math.floor(start / (timeFrame * Math.pow(10, units)));
  const endIndex = Math.ceil(end / (timeFrame * Math.pow(10, units)));

  const entries = [];
  for (let i = startIndex; i <= endIndex; i++) {
    const entry = i * timeFrame * Math.pow(10, units);
    entries.push(entry);
  }

  return entries;
}

```

This function takes three arguments: `` start ``, `` end ``, and `` timeFrame ``. It calculates the number of time units to use in the calculation by dividing the difference between the start and end times by the time frame, and then multiplying by the logarithm of the number of time units required to represent the time difference.

The function then calculates the starting and ending indices of the time units by dividing the start and end times by the product of the time frame and the logarithm, respectively.

The function then iterates over the range of indices and calculates the corresponding time units, adding them to the `` entries `` array.

The result is an array of time units that can be used to generate a time series between the start and end timestamps.

