To cut an arbitrary extension off a file name string in C#, you can use a regular expression to match the extension and then remove it from the string.

Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to do this:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string filePath = "myfile.txt.pdf";

        // Use a regular expression to match the extension
        string pattern = @"\.[^.]+";
        Match match = Regex.Match(filePath, pattern);

        if (match.Success)
        {
            // If the match is successful, the extension is captured in a group
            string extension = match.Groups[1].Value;

            // Remove the extension from the file path
            filePath = filePath.Replace(match.Value, "");

            Console.WriteLine("File path with extension removed: " + filePath);
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("No extension found in file path");
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we first define a regular expression pattern that matches any sequence of characters that end with a dot (.), followed by one or more characters that are not a dot. This pattern will match the `.pdf` extension in the `filePath` string.

Next, we use the `Regex.Match` method to search for a match in the `filePath` string using the `pattern` regular expression. If the match is successful, we capture the extension in a group using `match.Groups[1].Value`. We then use the `Replace` method to remove the extension from the `filePath` string by replacing it with an empty string. Finally, we print the resulting file path with the extension removed.

Note that this code assumes that the file path string only contains one extension. If the file path could contain multiple extensions, you would need to modify the regular expression pattern to match all of them.

