One way to resolve two components with the same name but one of them extending the first one is to use a combination of the following:

1. Use a different name for the extended component.
2. Use a different namespace for the extended component.
3. Import the extended component using the namespace.
4. When importing the component, use the fully qualified name (FQN) of the component to avoid conflicts.

An example of using FQN:
```javascript
// app.module.ts
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { BrowserModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { AppComponent } from './app.component';
import { extended-app-component } from './extended-app-component/extended-app-component';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [
    AppComponent,
    extendedAppComponent
  ],
  imports: [
    BrowserModule
  ],
  providers: [],
  bootstrap: [AppComponent]
})
export class AppModule { }
```

```javascript
// app.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    <app-extended-app-component></app-extended-app-component>
  `
})
export class AppComponent {}
```

```javascript
// extended-app-component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-extended-app-component',
  template: `
    <h1>Extended App Component</h1>
  `
})
export class ExtendedAppComponent extends AppComponent { }
```
In this example, the `AppComponent` is importing the `extended-app-component` using the fully qualified name `extendedAppComponent`, avoiding a conflict with the default `AppComponent` that is also imported in `app.module.ts`.

