Sure, here's a quicksort implementation in JavaScript:
```javascript
function quickSort(array) {
  if (array.length <= 1) {
    return array;
  }

  const pivot = array[0];
  const left = [];
  const right = [];

  for (let i = 1; i < array.length; i++) {
    if (array[i] < pivot) {
      left.push(array[i]);
    } else {
      right.push(array[i]);
    }
  }

  return [...quickSort(left), pivot, ...quickSort(right)];
}
```
This implementation uses the "partitioning" approach to quicksort, which involves dividing the array into two subarrays around the pivot element. The left subarray contains all elements that are less than the pivot, and the right subarray contains all elements that are greater than or equal to the pivot. The pivot element itself is not included in either subarray.

The function then recursively sorts the left and right subarrays using the quicksort function, and returns the concatenation of the sorted left subarray, the pivot element, and the sorted right subarray.

You can call this function with an array of integers as follows:
```javascript
const arr = [5, 3, 8, 1, 4, 7, 9];
const sortedArr = quickSort(arr);
console.log(sortedArr); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]
```

