One way to log all the calls being made to your ASP.Net website's SOAP endpoint is to use an interceptor. An interceptor is a component that can be added to the SOAP message pipeline, which allows you to intercept and modify messages as they are sent and received.

Here's an example of how you could implement an interceptor in your ASP.Net website:

1. Create a new class that implements the `System.Web.Services.Protocols.Soap12Interceptor` interface.
2. In the `Soap12Interceptor` class, override the `ProcessRequest` method. This method will be called for each SOAP request that is sent to your web service. In this method, you can log the request as desired (e.g. by writing it to a file or database).
3. In your web.config file, add the following configuration to the `<system.web.services>` element to register your interceptor:
```php
<interceptors>
  <add name="LoggingInterceptor" type="Namespace.LoggingInterceptor, AssemblyName" />
</interceptors>
```
4. Make sure to add the namespace and assembly name correctly in the above code snippet.

Here's an example of what the `LoggingInterceptor` class might look like:
```scss
using System.Web.Services.Protocols;

namespace Namespace
{
    public class LoggingInterceptor : Soap12Interceptor
    {
        public override void ProcessRequest(Soap12Message message)
        {
            // Log the request here
            // ...

            base.ProcessRequest(message);
        }
    }
}
```
By adding an interceptor to your SOAP endpoint, you can log all the requests made to your web service on the server-side.

