```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

type Map {
    Value interface{}
}

func main() {
    // create a channel to communicate between the main thread and the Goroutine
    channel := make(chan Map)

    // create a Goroutine that reads the map and prints it
    go func() {
        for {
            select {
            case m := <-channel:
                fmt.Println(m.Value)
            }
        }
    }()

    // create a Goroutine that writes to the map
    go func() {
        for {
            select {
            case g := <-channel:
                *g.Value = "new value"
                channel <- g
            }
        }
    }()
}

```

In this example, the `` Map `` struct is used to store the values of the map. The main thread uses a channel to communicate with the Goroutines. In the first Goroutine, the `` select `` statement is used to continuously read the values from the channel. In the second Goroutine, the `` select `` statement is used to continuously write to the channel.

Note that the `` channel `` variable is a global variable, so it can be used by both Goroutines. The `` select `` statement is used to handle the reading and writing of the channel, so that both Goroutines can use it at the same time.

This program is a simple example of how to use a channel for concurrency control and how to use Goroutines to modify a map. In a real-world scenario, you would likely want to use a more complex structure and more advanced concurrency control techniques.

