To create a custom plugin in Vite, you need to define a function that exports an object with specific properties and methods. Here's an example of how you could create a plugin that modifies files:
```js
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    {
      name: 'my-plugin',
      async transform(compiler) {
        const files = compiler.files;
        // Modify files here
        compiler.hooks.done.push((callback) => {
          // Callback function to signal that the transformation is complete
          callback();
        });
        return files;
      }
    }
  ]
});
```
In this example, the plugin is named `my-plugin`. The `transform` method is called by Vite when the plugin is loaded, and it receives an instance of the `Compiler` class as an argument. You can use this `Compiler` instance to access the list of files that will be processed by the plugin.

To modify the files, you can use the `fs` module that is built-in to Node.js. For example, you could change the contents of a file or add a new file to the compilation. Once you have finished modifying the files, you can call the `callback` function to signal that the transformation is complete.

You can then use this plugin in another plugin or in your main configuration by including it in the `plugins` array:
```js
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import myPlugin from './my-plugin';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [myPlugin]
});
```
Note that this is just an example, and you may need to modify the code to suit your specific use case.

