The main differences between var, let, and const in JavaScript are:

• var: 
- Has function scope. Variables declared with var are hoisted to the top of the function.
- Can be redeclared in the same scope.
- Can be reassigned different values.

For example:
```js
function foo() {
  var x = 1;
  var x = 2; // redeclaration is allowed
  x = 3; // reassignment is allowed
}
```

• let: 
- Has block scope. Variables declared with let are not hoisted. 
- Cannot be redeclared in the same scope. 
- Can be reassigned different values.

For example:
```js
function foo() {
  let x = 1;
  let x = 2; // redeclaration is NOT allowed
  x = 3; // reassignment IS allowed
}
```

• const: 
- Has block scope. 
- Cannot be redeclared or reassigned. Constant values remain the same.

For example:
```js
function foo() {
  const x = 1;
  const x = 2; // redeclaration is NOT allowed
  x = 3; // reassignment is NOT allowed 
}
```

Some examples of using them:

```js
// var
var x = 1;

// let
let y = 2;

// const 
const z = 3; 

// reassignment
x = 4; // allowed
y = 5; // allowed
z = 6; // not allowed

// redeclaration
var x = 7; // allowed
let y = 8; // not allowed 
const z = 9; // not allowed
```

In summary, use let over var whenever possible, and use const for values that will not change. This will make your code cleaner and more bug-free!

