To store relationships efficiently in CouchDB, you can use the `` attachments `` feature of the `` Document `` class. With this feature, you can attach other documents to a document, and then use the URLs of those documents in your application logic.

Here's an example of how to use the `` attachments `` feature to store a relationship between two documents:


```javascript
const doc1 = new Document({
    _id: 'doc1',
    title: 'Document 1',
    attachments: {
        'doc2': new Document({
            _id: 'doc2',
            title: 'Document 2',
        })
    }
});

const doc2 = new Document({
    _id: 'doc2',
    title: 'Document 2',
});

doc1.save();
doc2.save();

```

In this example, the `` attachments `` object of `` doc1 `` contains a single `` doc2 `` object, which itself contains a `` _id `` and a `` title ``. The `` _id `` and `` title `` of `` doc2 `` will be used as the key-value pair in the relationship between `` doc1 `` and `` doc2 ``.

To access the relationship between `` doc1 `` and `` doc2 ``, you can use the `` attachments `` property of `` doc1 `` like this:


```javascript
const doc1 = db.openDoc('doc1');
const doc2 = db.openDoc('doc2');

const relationship = doc1.attachments.doc2;

```

Once you have the relationship, you can use it to access the content of `` doc2 `` through the `` get `` method, like this:


```javascript
const doc2Content = doc1.attachments.doc2.get('doc2');

```

It's important to note that the `` attachments `` feature is not a replacement for proper data modeling and design, and in many cases, it may not be the most efficient way to store relationships between documents. However, it can be a useful tool for simple, one-to-one relationships where the documents are not part of a complex data model.

