To detect trendlines in stock data, you can use a simple linear regression approach. This involves finding the best-fitting line through the given data points. Here's a JavaScript function that does this:
```javascript
function detectTrendline(data) {
  // Calculate the slope and intercept of the best-fitting line
  const slope = calculateSlope(data);
  const intercept = calculateIntercept(slope);

  // Define the trendline equation y = mx + b
  const trendlineEquation = function (x) {
    return slope * x + intercept;
  };

  // Apply the trendline to the given data
  const trendedData = data.map((point) => [point[0], trendlineEquation(point[0])]);

  return {
    slope: slope,
    intercept: intercept,
    trendedData: trendedData,
  };
}

function calculateSlope(data) {
  const xValues = data.map((point) => point[0]);
  const yValues = data.map((point) => point[1]);

  const slopeSum = (yValues.reduce((acc, val) => acc + val, 0) - xValues.reduce((acc, val) => acc + val, 0)) / (xValues.length - 1);
  const interceptSum = xValues.reduce((acc, val) => acc + val, 0) - slopeSum;

  return slopeSum;
}

function calculateIntercept(slope) {
  return -(slope / (1 - (slope ? 1 : 0)));
}
```
This function takes an array of stock data points (an array of arrays, where each inner array contains [date, price]) and returns the trendline's slope, intercept, and the trended data.

Keep in mind that this is a simple linear regression approach and may not be the best choice for complex stock market data. More advanced techniques like moving averages or RSI might be more suitable for detecting trends in stock prices.

