Here is a basic bubble sort algorithm in JavaScript:

```js
function bubbleSort(array) {
  let swapped = true;

  while (swapped) {
    swapped = false;

    for (let i = 0; i < array.length - 1; i++) {
      if (array[i] > array[i + 1]) {
        [array[i], array[i + 1]] = [array[i + 1], array[i]];
        swapped = true;
      }
    }
  }

  return array; 
}

let array = [5, 3, 2, 1, 4];
bubbleSort(array);
// [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

This performs bubble sort on the array. It iterates over the array multiple times, comparing adjacent elements and swapping them if they are in the wrong order. It continues iterating until no swaps are needed, meaning the array is sorted.

The time complexity of bubble sort is O(n2) since we have nested loops. The space complexity is O(1) since we sort the array in-place.

Bubble sort is not very efficient but is a simple algorithm to understand and implement. Let me know if you have any questions or need an explanation of another sorting algorithm!

