There are a few ways to suppress undefined index warnings in PHP without changing php.ini:

1. Use the @ suppression operator:
```php
$foo = @$_GET['bar'];
```
This will suppress any warnings generated by the $_GET['bar'] access.

2. Use the error_reporting() function to temporarily lower the error reporting level:
```php 
error_reporting(E_ALL ^ E_NOTICE);
$foo = $_GET['bar'];
error_reporting(E_ALL);
``` 
This will suppress E_NOTICE (which includes undefined index warnings) just for that block of code.

3. Use the set_error_handler() function to define a custom error handler that ignores undefined index warnings:
```php
set_error_handler("myErrorHandler");

function myErrorHandler($errno, $errstr, $errfile, $errline) 
{
    if ($errno == E_NOTICE && strpos($errstr, "Undefined index") !== false) {
        return;
    }

    // Default error handler
    return false; 
}

$foo = $_GET['bar'];
``` 
This custom error handler will ignore undefined index warnings, suppressing them.

4. Use the null coalescing operator ?? to provide a default value: 
```php
$foo = $_GET['bar'] ?? 'default value';
```
This will return the value of $_GET['bar'] if it exists, or 'default value' if not, suppressing the warning.

