`map()` and `flatMap()` are two methods in Java Stream API that are used to transform and manipulate collections of data.

The main difference between `map()` and `flatMap()` is that `map()` is used to transform each element of a collection into a new element, while `flatMap()` is used to transform each element into a sequence of elements, flattening the stream of nested collections into a single collection.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference between `map()` and `flatMap()`:
```java
NAME_1<String> NAME_1 = Arrays.asList("A", "B", "C", "D", "E");

NAME_1<String> flattenedList = NAME_1.stream().flatMap(item -> {
    if (item instanceof NAME_1) {
        return ((NAME_1<String>) item).stream();
    } else {
        return Stream.of(item);
    }
}).collect(Collectors.toList());

NAME_1<String> mappedList = NAME_1.stream().map(item -> {
    if (item instanceof NAME_1) {
        NAME_1<String> subList = ((NAME_1<String>) item).stream().map(subItem -> {
            if (subItem instanceof NAME_1) {
                return ((NAME_1<String>) subItem).stream();
            } else {
                return subItem;
            }
        }).collect(Collectors.toList());
        return subList;
    } else {
        return item;
    }
}).collect(Collectors.toList());
```
In this example, we have a NAME_1 of strings `NAME_1` which contains nested lists. We use `flatMap()` to flatten the stream of nested lists into a single NAME_1, while `map()` is used to transform each element into a nested NAME_1 of strings.

You can see that `flatMap()` is used to transform each element into a sequence of elements, while `map()` is used to transform each element into a new element with the same type.

So, in summary, `map()` is used to transform each element into a new element, while `flatMap()` is used to transform each element into a sequence of elements, flattening the stream of nested collections into a single collection.

