Sure! Here's an example of a simple task scheduler in JavaScript:

```javascript
const taskScheduler = (tasks) => {
  const scheduledTasks = [];

  const enqueue = (task) => {
    scheduledTasks.push(task);
  };

  const start = () => {
    const taskToRun = scheduledTasks.shift();
    if (taskToRun) {
      taskToRun.run();
      setInterval(start, 1000);
    }
  };

  return {
    enqueue,
    start
  };
};
```

This scheduler uses a `scheduledTasks` array to keep track of the tasks that have been enqueued but not yet run. The `enqueue` method adds a task to this array, and the `start` method runs the first task in the array, then starts the scheduler again every second.
Here's an example of how you could use this scheduler:

```javascript
const taskScheduler = (tasks) => {
  //...
};

const task1 = {
  run: () => {
    console.log('Task 1 has run.');
  }
};

const task2 = {
  run: () => {
    console.log('Task 2 has run.');
  }
};

taskScheduler.enqueue(task1);
taskScheduler.enqueue(task2);

taskScheduler.start();
```

In this example, we create two tasks and enqueue them in the scheduler. When the `start` method is called, it runs the first task in the `scheduledTasks` array (in this case, `task1`), then starts the scheduler again every second. After `task1` runs, the scheduler runs `task2`, then starts the scheduler again, and so on.

