Here's the code for the recursive Hanoi puzzle solution in JavaScript:
```javascript
function Hanoi(state, size) {
  if (state.length === size) {
    return [state];
  }

  const halfSize = size / 2;
  const steps = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < size; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < size; j++) {
      steps.push({
        x: i,
        y: j,
        move: state[i][j],
      });
    }
  }

  steps.sort((a, b) => a.move - b.move);

  const solution = [];

  while (steps.length > 0) {
    for (let i = 0; i < steps.length; i++) {
      const move = steps[i].move;
      const temp = steps.splice(i, 1)[0];
      solution.unshift([...state, steps[i].x, steps[i].y, move, temp]);
    }
  }

  return solution;
}
```
This function takes two arguments: the current state of the Hanoi puzzle and the size of the puzzle. It returns an array of arrays representing the sequence of moves required to solve the puzzle.

Sure, here's a simple implementation of a priority queue using JavaScript:

```javascript
// Create a class for the elements inside the queue
class QueueElement {
    constructor(element, priority) {
        this.element = element; // The actual element/data
        this.priority = priority; // The priority of the element
    }
}

// Create a class for the Priority Queue
class PriorityQueue {
    constructor() {
        this.items = []; // Initialize an empty array to store the queue elements
    }

    // Function to add an element to the queue with its priority
    enqueue(element, priority) {
        const queueElement = new QueueElement(element, priority); 
        let added = false;

        // Looping through the entire item array to add element at the correct location of the Queue
        for (let i = 0; i < this.items.length; i++) {
            if (this.items[i].priority > queueElement.priority) {
                this.items.splice(i, 0, queueElement); // Insert the new element at the right position
                added = true;
                break;
            }
        }

        // If the element has the highest priority, it is added at the end of the queue
        if (!added) {
            this.items.push(queueElement);
        }
    }

    // Function to remove an element from the queue
    dequeue() {
        if (this.isEmpty()) {
            return "Underflow"; // If the queue is empty, there is no element to dequeue
        }
        return this.items.shift(); // Removes the highest priority element (first element) from the array
    }

    // Function to check if the queue is empty
    isEmpty() {
        return this.items.length === 0;
    }

    // Function to get the element with the highest priority
    front() {
        if (this.isEmpty()) {
            return "No elements in the queue"; // If the queue is empty, there is no front element
        }
        return this.items[0]; // Return the element with the highest priority
    }

    // Function to check the length of the queue
    size() {
        return this.items.length;
    }
}
```
This code creates a basic priority queue with the methods enqueue, dequeue, isEmpty, front, and size. The priority queue sorts elements by priority where the lower the priority number, the higher the priority of the element. You can add elements to the queue with a priority, remove elements, check if the queue is empty, get the front element and get the size of the queue.

