The Kruskal algorithm is a greedy algorithm used to find the minimum spanning tree (MST) of a connected, undirected graph. It starts with an empty set of edges and iteratively adds the smallest edge that connects two separate components of the graph until all vertices are included in a tree.

Here are the steps of the Kruskal algorithm:

1. Sort all the edges of the graph in ascending order of their weight (or cost).
2. Initialize an empty set of edges.
3. For each edge in the sorted list, check if it connects two separate components of the graph. If so, add it to the set of edges.
4. Repeat step 3 until all vertices are included in a tree.

The resulting spanning tree is the minimum spanning tree of the graph.

Here is a C++ code example of the Kruskal algorithm:
