In C++, you can use the `std::wstring` class to represent wide characters strings, and you can convert a `std::string` to a `std::wstring` using the `std::wstring::from` function. Here's an example:
```c
#include <string>
#include <wstring>

int main() {
    std::string s = "Hello, world!";
    std::wstring w = std::wstring::from(s);
    return 0;
}
```
This will create a wide characters string `w` that represents the same text as the `std::string` `s`. Note that the wide characters string is constructed by copying the characters of the narrow string `s`, so any changes made to the wide characters string will not affect the narrow string and vice versa.

If you don't want to use `std::wstring::from()` to convert a `std::string` to a `std::wstring`, you can manually copy the characters of the string using a loop and the `std::char_traits<char>` class. Here's an example:
```c
#include <string>
#include <wstring>

int main() {
    std::string s = "Hello, world!";
    std::wstring w;
    for (char c : s) {
        w.push_back(std::char_traits<char>::tolower(c));
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This code creates a wide characters string `w` by iterating through the characters of the narrow string `s` and converting each character to its lowercase equivalent using `std::char_traits<char>::tolower()`. The resulting wide characters string represents the same text as the narrow string, but in wide characters format.

