You can extend multiple classes in Kotlin using the : symbol. For example:
```kotlin
open class A 
open class B

class C : A(), B() {
    // ...
}
```
This declares a C class that extends both A and B.

You can also extend classes and implement interfaces at the same time:
```kotlin
interface I 
open class A

class C : I, A() {
    // ...
}
```

When extending multiple types, you need to call the primary constructor of each superclass and interface. So if A and B both have constructors, you need to call them both:
```kotlin
open class A(val a: Int) 
open class B(val b: Int)

class C(a: Int, b: Int) : A(a), B(b) {
    // ...
}
```

You can also have a primary constructor in the derived class, and call the superconstructor from there:
```kotlin
open class A(val a: Int) 
open class B(val b: Int)

class C : A, B {
    constructor(a: Int, b: Int) : super(a), super(b)
} 
```

