To add a delay to a CSS animation, you can use the animation-delay property.

For example, to add a 2 second delay to your blink animation, you can do:

```css
animation: blink 6s ease-out 2s; 
```

Or alternatively:

```css
animation: blink 6s ease-out;
animation-delay: 2s;
```

So your full code would be:

```css
.blink {
  animation: blink 6s ease-out 2s;
}

@keyframes blink {
  0% { opacity: 1; }
  50% { opacity: 0; }
  100% { opacity: 1; }
}
```

This will wait 2 seconds after the element is loaded, and then start the blinking animation, blinking for 6 seconds.

You can use any time value for the animation-delay:

- Seconds: 2s 
- Milliseconds: 200ms
- Minutes: 1m 

Etc.

Some other examples:

```css
/* Wait 1 second */ 
animation-delay: 1s;  

/* Wait half a second */
animation-delay: 500ms;  

/* Wait 10 minutes */ 
animation-delay: 10m; 
```

So animation-delay is a useful way to add pauses and delays to your CSS animations for more customized and complex timing effects.

