Yes, you can absolutely use color gradients for text in CSS! There are a few ways to do this:

1. Use a gradient as the text fill:

```css
.text {
  color: transparent;
  -webkit-text-fill-color: linear-gradient(to right, red, yellow); 
}
```

This will fill the text with a red to yellow gradient.

2. Use a gradient as the text stroke, with a transparent fill:

```css 
.text {
  color: transparent;
  -webkit-text-stroke: 2px linear-gradient(to right, red, yellow);
}
```

This will stroke the text with a 2px red to yellow gradient, with the text fill transparent.

3. Use a gradient for the text shadow:

```css
.text {
  color: #fff;
  text-shadow: 
    -1px -1px 0 linear-gradient(to right, red, yellow),
     1px -1px 0 linear-gradient(to right, red, yellow),
    -1px 1px 0 linear-gradient(to right, red, yellow),
     1px 1px 0 linear-gradient(to right, red, yellow); 
}
```

This will apply 4 text shadows with the red to yellow gradient, giving a gradient effect.

4. Apply a gradient mask to the text:

```css
.text {
  -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, transparent, #000 75%);
  color: #fff; 
}
```

This will mask the text so only 75% is shown, with a gradient.

