The EventSource API can be used to get streaming data from a server. Here's how you can use it:

1. On the server, send data in text/event-stream format. For example:

```
data: {"message": "Hello"}

data: {"message": "World"}
```

2. On the client, create a new EventSource, passing it the URL to stream from:

```js
const source = new EventSource('/stream');
```

3. Attach event listeners to the EventSource for the events you want to handle:

```js 
source.addEventListener('message', (e) => {
  console.log(e.data); // Prints the data from the server
}, false);
```

4. The eventsource will automatically connect to the server and keep the connection open, firing events as data is received.

5. To close the connection, call `source.close()`.

So a full example would be:

Server:
```
const stream = require('stream');
const server = require('http').createServer();

server.on('request', (req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, {
    'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream',
    'Cache-Control': 'no-cache'
  });

  const es = new stream.Readable();
  es.push('data: {"message": "Hello"}\n\n');
  es.push('data: {"message": "World"}\n\n');
  es.pipe(res);
});

server.listen(8000); 
```

Client:
```js
const source = new EventSource('http://localhost:8000');

source.addEventListener('message', (e) => {
  console.log(e.data);
}, false);
```

This will print:
{message: "Hello"} 
{message: "World"}

