```c
#include <stdint.h>

void write_leb128(int32_t value, FILE *stream) {
    uint32_t uvalue = value;
    unsigned char byte;

    do {
        byte = uvalue & 0x7F;
        uvalue >>= 7;

        if (uvalue != ((value < 0) && (byte & 0x40))) {
            byte |= 0x80;  /* more bytes to come */
        }

        fwrite(&byte, sizeof(byte), 1, stream);
    } while (uvalue);
}
```

This function uses bitwise operations to encode the input integer. It shifts the value right by 7 bits and if the value is still non-zero, it sets the most significant bit of the current byte to indicate that there are more bytes to follow. The bytes are written to the given file stream.

