The #include <queue> directive in C++ includes the queue header file. This allows you to use the queue container in your C++ program.

The queue is a first-in, first-out (FIFO) container adapter. It has two ends - front and back. New elements are added at the back end (enqueue operation) and removed from the front end (dequeue operation).

Some common operations on queues are:

- queue<int> q; - Creates an empty queue of ints
- q.push(x); - Adds an element x at the back of the queue
- q.pop(); - Removes the front element 
- q.front(); - Gets the front element 
- q.back(); - Gets the last element 
- q.empty(); - Checks if the queue is empty
- q.size(); - Gets the number of elements in the queue

For example:
```cpp
#include <queue>

int main() {
  queue<int> q;

  // Add elements to the queue
  q.push(1);
  q.push(2);
  q.push(3);

  // Print front element
  std::cout << q.front() << '\n';  // 1

  // Remove front element
  q.pop();

  // Print new front element
  std::cout << q.front() << '\n';  // 2

  // Check if empty
  if (q.empty()) {
    std::cout << "Queue is empty!\n";
  } else {
    std::cout << "Queue size: " << q.size() << '\n';  // 2
  }
}
```

