```c
#include <vector>

// Function to compute the mean squared error between a batch of predictions and a batch of ground truth
double mse(std::vector<double> predictions, std::vector<double> groundTruth) {
    double total = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < predictions.size(); i++) {
        total += predictions[i] - groundTruth[i] * 2.0;
    }
    return total / predictions.size();
}
```
This function takes in two vectors of doubles, `predictions` and `groundTruth`, which represent the batch of predictions and ground truth respectively. It computes the mean squared error by iterating through each element in the `predictions` vector and subtracting the corresponding element in the `groundTruth` vector from it. The result is then divided by the number of elements in the `predictions` vector to obtain the mean squared error.

To write an interval in React.js, you can use the `setInterval()` or `setTimeout()` method.

`setInterval()` method is used to execute a function at regular intervals of time. It takes two arguments, the first is the function to be executed and the second is the time interval in milliseconds.

Example:
```javascript
setInterval(() => {
  console.log('Hello World!');
}, 1000);
```
This will log "Hello World!" every 1000 milliseconds (1 second).

`setTimeout()` method is used to execute a function after a specified time interval. It also takes two arguments, the first is the function to be executed and the second is the time interval in milliseconds.

Example:
```javascript
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log('Hello World!');
}, 1000);
```
This will log "Hello World!" after 1000 milliseconds (1 second).

You can also use `clearInterval()` and `clearTimeout()` methods to stop the interval or timeout respectively.

Example:
```javascript
let intervalId = setInterval(() => {
  console.log('Hello World!');
}, 1000);

// Stop the interval after 2 seconds
clearInterval(intervalId);
```

