To pass a JSON object to the front-end in a Next.js application, you can send it as a response object in the `get` handler function of the route.

Here's an example:
```less
app.get('/', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    // Get data from a database or API
    const data = await getData();

    // Send the data as a JSON response
    res.status(200).json(data);
  } catch (error) {
    // Handle errors
    res.status(500).send('Error getting data');
  }
});
```
In the front-end, you can use the `fetch` API to make a GET request to the `/` route and parse the response as JSON:
```javascript
fetch('/')
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => {
    // Use the data in your application
  });
```
Note that you should always validate and sanitize the data before sending it to the front-end to prevent security vulnerabilities.

