An "object reference error" (also known as a "NullReferenceException") occurs when you try to call a method or access a property of an object that is `null` or not set.

Here are some common reasons why you might get an object reference error:

1. You are trying to call a method or access a property of an object that is `null`. You can check if an object is `null` using the `null` operator (`null` coalescing operator) or the `Is` operator.
2. You are trying to call a method or access a property of an object that has not been set. Make sure you initialize the object before calling its methods or accessing its properties.
3. You are trying to call a method or access a property of an object that has been set to `null` after it was first created. Make sure you do not modify the object after it has been created if you need to keep using it.

Here is an example of how you might fix an object reference error:
```csharp
class MyClass
{
    public int x;

    public void DoSomething()
    {
        // This will cause an object reference error if x is null
        Console.WriteLine(x * 2);
    }
}

// Call the DoSomething method and assign null to x
MyClass obj = new MyClass();
obj.x = null;
obj.DoSomething(); // This will cause an object reference error

