obj.DoSomething(); // This will not cause an object reference error
```
In this example, the `DoSomething` method tries to access the `x` field of the `MyClass` object, but if `x` is `null`, the method will throw an object reference error.

To fix the error, you can either check if `x` is `null` before calling the method, or you can make sure that `x` is initialized to a non-null value before calling the method. In this case, you can initialize `x` to `10` instead of `null`, which will make the method call succeed without throwing an object reference error.

