To check if a file is hit by a `gitignore` file in JavaScript, you can use the `fs.readFile()` method to read the contents of the file and then check if the contents match any of the patterns in the `gitignore` file. Here's an example:
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const gitignore = 'path/to/gitignore';
const filePath = 'path/to/file';
fs.readFile(filePath, (err, data) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  const contents = data.toString();
  const gitignoreContents = fs.readFileSync(gitignore, 'utf8');
  if (contents.match(gitignoreContents)) {
    console.log('This file is ignored by the gitignore.');
  } else {
    console.log('This file is not ignored by the gitignore.');
  }
});
```
In this example, `filePath` is the path to the file you want to check, and `gitignore` is the path to the `gitignore` file. The `fs.readFile()` method reads the contents of the file at `filePath` and returns it as a string. The `fs.readFileSync()` method reads the contents of the `gitignore` file at `gitignore` and returns it as a string.
Then, the `match()` method is used to check if the contents of the file at `filePath` match any of the patterns in the contents of the `gitignore` file. If the contents match any of the patterns, the console log will say that the file is ignored by the `gitignore`. Otherwise, the console log will say that the file is not ignored by the `gitignore`.

