Here is a simple asynchronous C# socket server that handles multiple connections concurrently and outputs the current date and time before disconnecting each client:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Sockets;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class SocketServer 
{
  public static async Task Main()
  {
    var listener = new TcpListener(IPAddress.Any, 8080);
    listener.Start();

    while (true) 
    {
      TcpClient client = await listener.AcceptTcpClientAsync();

      _ = HandleClientAsync(client);
    }
  }

  private static async Task HandleClientAsync(TcpClient client)
  {
    try 
    {
      NetworkStream stream = client.GetStream();

      byte[] date = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(DateTime.Now.ToString("r"));
      await stream.WriteAsync(date, 0, date.Length);

      client.Close();
    }
    catch 
    {
      // ignore exceptions on close
    }
  }
}
```

This creates a TcpListener, accepts incoming connections in a loop, and passes each TcpClient to the HandleClientAsync method. This method gets the NetworkStream, writes the current date/time, closes the connection, and returns. The asynchronous operations allow it to handle multiple concurrent connections.

