Here is a quicksort implementation in C++:
```cpp
void quicksort(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    if (low < high) {
        int pivot = partition(arr, low, high);

        quicksort(arr, low, pivot-1);
        quicksort(arr, pivot+1, high);
    }
}

int partition(int arr[], int low, int high) {
    int pivot = arr[high];
    int i = low - 1;

    for (int j = low; j <= high - 1; j++) {
        if (arr[j] < pivot) {
            i++;
            swap(arr[i], arr[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(arr[i+1], arr[high]);
    return i + 1;
}

void swap(int &a, int &b) {
    int temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp; 
}
```

This implements the quicksort algorithm to sort an array. The quicksort function calls itself recursively to sort the left and right halves of the array. The partition function picks a pivot element and partitions the array around the pivot.

