Yes, here is an example of a Telegram bot written in Node.js that can delete all messages containing specific words:

First, you will need to install the required packages:
```
npm install telegram-bot
npm install cheerio
```
Then, create a new file and add the following code:
```javascript
const TelegramBot = require('telegram-bot');
const cheerio = require('cheerio');

const bot = new TelegramBot('YOUR_BOT_TOKEN');

bot.onText(/\/start\//, (msg, match) => {
  bot.sendMessage(msg.from, 'Welcome to the group! Please enter the word you want to search for.');
  bot.on('message', (msg) => {
    const $ = cheerio.load(msg.text);
    const searchWord = msg.text.match(/(\w+)/)[1];
    const messagesToDelete = $(`span.message_text:contains(${searchWord})`).parent();
    messagesToDelete.remove();
    bot.sendMessage(msg.from, `Successfully deleted messages containing ${searchWord}.`);
  });
});

bot.start();
```
This code creates a Telegram bot that listens for the command `/start`. When the command is given, the bot will prompt the user to enter a word to search for. The bot will then search all messages in the group for the specified word and delete any that are found. The deleted messages will be removed permanently and will not be retrieved from the Telegram servers.

To deploy this bot, you will need to have a Telegram bot token. You can get one by following the instructions on the Telegram website. Once you have a token, replace `'YOUR_BOT_TOKEN'` in the code above with your actual bot token.

After you have created the bot, you can run it locally using the Node.js command:
```
node index.js
```
