TypeScript is a typed superset of JavaScript that compiles to plain JavaScript. It adds static typing and class-based object-oriented programming to JavaScript.

Some of the main benefits of TypeScript are:

1. Static typing - TypeScript adds static typing to JavaScript. This means that you can specify the types of variables, function parameters and object properties. This allows you to catch errors at compile time rather than run time.

2. Interfaces - TypeScript allows you to define interfaces which act as contracts in your code. This allows you to model complex types in your applications.

3. Classes - TypeScript adds class-based object oriented programming to JavaScript. You can define classes, inheritance, interfaces, etc. This structures your code in a familiar way.

4. Compiles to JavaScript - TypeScript compiles to plain JavaScript code which can run in any browser, any host and any OS. So you get the benefits of static typing and object oriented programming without changing your runtime environment.

5. Great tooling - TypeScript has excellent editor support. You get autocompletion, inline errors, and rich type information in editors like VS Code.

