Sure! Here's a JavaScript function that takes in a string and returns a new string with alternating capitalization:


```javascript
function alternateCapitalization(str) {
  let i = 0;
  let j = 0;
  let result = "";

  while (i < str.length && j < str.length) {
    if (i % 2 === 0) {
      result += str[i].toUpperCase();
      j++;
    } else {
      result += str[i].toLowerCase();
      i++;
    }
  }

  while (i < str.length) {
    result += str[i].toLowerCase();
    i++;
  }

  while (j < str.length) {
    result += str[j].toUpperCase();
    j++;
  }

  return result;
}

```

This function uses a `` while `` loop to iterate over the characters in the input string. It checks the index of the current character using the modulo operator (`` % ``) to determine whether it is odd or even. If it is odd, it capitalizes the character using the `` toUpperCase() `` method, otherwise it uses the `` toLowerCase() `` method. The output string is built using the `` result `` variable, and the function continues until the input string is exhausted. Finally, the `` result `` string is returned.

Here's an example of handling multiple I/O operations in Golang:
```go
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "io/ioutil"
    "net/http"
)

func main() {
    // Make two channels to handle IO operations
    c1 := make(chan string)
    c2 := make(chan string)

    // Launch two goroutines to handle IO
    go func() {
        resp, _ := http.Get("http://example.com")
        body, _ := ioutil.ReadAll(resp.Body)
        c1 <- string(body)
    }()

    go func() {
        resp, _ := http.Get("http://golang.org") 
        body, _ := ioutil.ReadAll(resp.Body)
        c2 <- string(body)
    }()

    // Receive results from two channels
    fmt.Println(<-c1)
    fmt.Println(<-c2)
}
```

This program:

- Makes two channels c1 and c2
- Launches two goroutines to make HTTP requests to example.com and golang.org
- Each goroutine reads the response and sends the result on its channel
- The main goroutine receives from both channels and prints the results
- This allows handling two I/O operations concurrently and waiting for both results

