This is a classic concurrency issue known as a race condition. There are a few ways to fix this:

1. Use locks/mutexes to serialize access to the critical section (the event invocation). This would look something like:

```csharp
lock (lockObj) 
{
    var handler = update.Type switch
    {
        // ...
    };
    await handler ?? Task.CompletedTask; 
}
```

This will ensure only one handler executes at a time, fixing the race condition.

2. Use asynchronous locks (SemaphoreSlim) for better performance:

```csharp
using var lockObj = new SemaphoreSlim(1);
await lockObj.WaitAsync(cancellationToken);
try 
{
    var handler = update.Type switch
    {
        // ...
    };
    await handler ?? Task.CompletedTask; 
}
finally 
{
    lockObj.Release(); 
}
```

3. Run each event handler invocation on a separate thread using Task.Run:

```csharp
Task.Run(async () => 
{
    var handler = update.Type switch
    {
        // ...
    };
    await handler ?? Task.CompletedTask; 
});
```

This will execute each handler concurrently but on separate threads, avoiding the race condition.

4. Use async/await and Task.WhenAll to run all event handlers concurrently but wait for all to complete:

