Yes, it is absolutely okay to use camelCase function names in PHP. In fact, it is recommended by the PHP coding standards.

Some reasons to use camelCase:

1. Consistency - Most PHP core functions and methods use camelCase. Using the same style makes your code consistent and familiar to other PHP developers.

2. Readability - camelCase is easier to read than underscore_case. It flows better and is more visually appealing.

3. Trend - The majority of open source PHP projects now use camelCase, so it has become the de facto standard.

Here's an example of camelCase in PHP:

```php
function getUserEmail() 
{
    // ...
}

$email = getUserEmail();
```

Compare this to underscore_case:

```php 
function get_user_email() 
{
    // ... 
}

$email = get_user_email();
```

So in summary, yes - camelCase is the preferred and recommended way to name functions in PHP. Stick to this convention and your PHP code will be consistent, readable and up to standards.

