The quicksort algorithm is a sorting algorithm that divides an input list into two smaller lists, sorts each of these smaller lists, and merges them into a single, sorted list. Here's an example of how to implement the quicksort algorithm in NAME_1:


```scss
public static int quickSort(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
    if (low >= high)
        return 0;
    int pivot = arr[low];
    int left = low;
    int right = high;
    while (left < right) {
        if (arr[left] < pivot) {
            int temp = arr[left];
            arr[left] = arr[right];
            arr[right] = temp;
            left++;
        } else {
            int temp = arr[right];
            arr[right] = arr[left];
            arr[left] = temp;
            right--;
        }
    }
    if (low < high)
        quickSort(arr, low, high);
    if (low < high)
        quickSort(arr, low, high);
    return low;
}

```

This example takes an input list of integers and a low and high index representing the bounds of the list to be sorted. It calculates the median of the list (the value at the midpoint of the low and high indices) and uses it as a pivot value. It then divides the list into two smaller lists, one containing elements less than the pivot and one containing elements greater than the pivot. It repeatedly compares the elements in each sublist and swaps them if necessary until the sublists are sorted. Once the sublists are sorted, the function merges the two sublists into a single, sorted list and returns the low index of the sorted list.

Note that this is just one possible implementation of the quicksort algorithm, and there are many other ways to implement it.

