In C++, you can use template specialization to achieve selective compilation of different implementations of a class. You can define a base class with a common interface and implement multiple specialized classes that provide different implementations for the base class's operations.

Here is an example of how you can use template specialization to achieve selective compilation of different implementations of a class:
```cpp
#include <iostream>

// Base class with a common interface
template <typename T>
class BaseClass {
public:
    virtual void foo() = 0;
};

// Specialized classes that provide different implementations of the base class's operations
template <typename T>
class SpecializedClassA : public BaseClass<T> {
public:
    void foo() {
        std::cout << "SpecializedClassA::foo()\n";
    }
};

template <typename T>
class SpecializedClassB : public BaseClass<T> {
public:
    void foo() {
        std::cout << "SpecializedClassB::foo()\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    // Create an instance of the specialized class
    BaseClass<int> bc;
    SpecializedClassA<int> sca;
    SpecializedClassB<int> scb;

    // Call the foo() function on the instances
    bc.foo();
    sca.foo();
    scb.foo();

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, `BaseClass` is a templated class that defines a common interface for all specialized classes. `SpecializedClassA` and `SpecializedClassB` are two specialized classes that provide different implementations of the `BaseClass` operations.

To use the different implementations of `BaseClass`, you can create instances of the specialized classes and call the `foo()` function on each instance.

Note that the use of template specialization allows the different implementations of the base class to be selected at compile time based on the type of the specialized class. This can be useful when you have multiple specialized classes that provide different implementations of the same operations, and you want to ensure that the correct implementation is used for each specialized class.

